NOTEXT	dr_diagrams-representing-the-relative-mortality-from-zymotic-diseases-blue-10564004	Diagrams Representing the Relative Mortality from Zymotic Diseases (Blue), from Wounds (red) (Eyre and Spottiswoode, 1858). No text derivative.
NOTEXT	dr_army-in-the-east-10564003	Army in the East (Eyre and Spottiswoode, 1858). No text derivative.
NOTEXT	dr_diagrams-constructed-on-the-data-furnished-by-the-quarter-master-generals-p-10564005	Diagrams Constructed on the Data Furnished by the Quarter Master General's Plans for Encampment (Eyre and Spottiswoode, 1858). No text derivative.
#
#   THIS MATTERS FOR THE STATED PRIORITY OF THIS BATCH. These four items are the coxcomb /
#   polar-area diagram plates themselves — the single most famous artefact she produced.
#   They survive as IMAGES ONLY and cannot enter a text corpus. What CAN be had, and was,
#   is the underlying arithmetic: mortality-british-army-1858 prints the tables the
#   diagrams were drawn from, including "Table K. fig. 1. Estimated Average, Monthly
#   Strength of the Army in the East, and the Deaths and Annual Rate of Mortality per 1000
#   in each month, from April 1854 to March 1856, from Zymotic Diseases, Wounds, and
#   Injuries; and all other Causes" — which is the coxcomb's data, month by month.
#   So the diagrams are absent but their numbers are present. Downstream should not expect
#   to find a description of the diagram; it should expect to find the table.
#
